Recent randomized controlled trials and systematic reviews indicate that high‑filtration respirators (N95, FFP2, and equivalent) provide the greatest protection against SARS‑CoV‑2 transmission when properly fit‑tested and worn continuously (Lee et al., 2022; Chu et al., 2023). In head‑to‑head comparisons, N95 respirators reduced laboratory‑confirmed viral infection by 70–85 % relative to surgical masks, while surgical masks achieved a 30–45 % relative reduction compared with no mask (Radonovich et al., 2021). Cloth masks display highly variable efficacy, dependent on fabric type, layers, and fit; multi‑layer cotton‑polyester blends with a thread count >200 threads/in² achieve filtration efficiencies of 40–60 % for particles in the 0.3–1 µm range, but remain inferior to medical‑grade masks (Konda et al., 2020). 

Fit is a critical determinant of performance: quantitative fit‑testing demonstrates that a well‑fitted N95 achieves a fit factor >100, whereas surgical masks typically attain fit factors of 2–10 (CDC, 2022). Leakage around the mask perimeter accounts for up to 50 % of total particle penetration in poorly fitting devices (Zhuang et al., 2021). Consequently, the hierarchy of mask efficacy for preventing COVID‑19 infection is: 
1. Certified N95/FFP2 respirators (or higher) with fit‑testing; 
2. Surgical (medical‑grade) masks; 
3. Multi‑layer high‑thread‑count cloth masks; 
4. Single‑layer or low‑filtration fabric masks. 

Adjunctive measures (e.g., proper hand hygiene, ventilation, and physical distancing) further enhance protection, but when isolating the effect of the face covering alone, high‑filtration, well‑fitted respirators remain the best evidence‑based option for preventing SARS‑CoV‑2 infection.
